text:
This series of studies presents the development of a measure of emotional intelligence based on the emotional intelligence model created by Salovey and Mayer (1990). Initially, a pool of 62 items was used to represent the various dimensions of the model. Through a factor analysis of responses from 346 participants, a 33-item scale was derived. Subsequent studies demonstrated that the 33-item measure exhibited strong internal consistency and test-retest reliability. Validation studies further revealed that scores on the 33-item measure: 

1. (a) exhibited correlations with eight out of nine theoretically related constructs, such as alexithymia, attention to feelings, clarity of feelings, mood repair, optimism, and impulse control;
2. (b) predicted first-year college grades;
3. (c) were significantly higher among therapists compared to therapy clients or prisoners;
4. (d) were significantly higher among females than males, consistent with previous findings in emotional skills studies;
5. (e) showed no relationship with cognitive ability; and
6. (f) were associated with the openness to experience trait within the big five personality dimensions.
